  "response": "## 1. The finding\n\n`crime_00020` — **Concurrency Stampede** (`unbounded_async_fanout`), medium / confidence 0.88, `test/utils.ts:38-46`, symbol `run → mkdir`. It's the only concurrency finding in the report, and it sits exactly on the helper CI is failing in — `blast_radius_direct_importers: 9`, i.e. nine test files call `run()`.\n\nIts evidence, verbatim, for the fan-out site and the collection it iterates:\n\n> - `\"fan-out: Promise.all at line 38\"`\n> - `\"collection: Object.keys(files).map(…)\"`\n> - `\"collection source: an awaited call (query, request, or listing)\"`\n> - `\"produced by: Object.keys\"`\n> - `\"per-element work (2):\"`\n>   - `\"filesystem operation — fsp.mkdir at line 43\"`\n>   - `\"filesystem operation — fsp.writeFile at line 44\"`\n> - `\"no bound visible: no `.slice()` or `take`/`limit` on the source, no batching, no concurrency-limit helper, no semaphore\"`\n> - `\"if the collection holds N elements, N concurrent filesystem operation start at once — N is a property of the data, not of this code\"`\n\nOne evidence caveat: `Object.keys(files)` is a synchronous read of the `files` parameter, so the `\"collection source: an awaited call\"` line (worth +0.14 of the confidence) looks like the detector attributing the source to the enclosing `await Promise.all(...)`. That softens the confidence slightly but not the conclusion — `files` is still supplied by each caller, so N is caller-controlled either way.\n\n## 2. Why the failure is load-dependent, and therefore machine-dependent\n\nThe last evidence line is the whole bug: **N is a property of the data, not of this code.** `fsp.writeFile` opens a descriptor, writes, closes. Every element is dispatched in the same tick, so the number of descriptors held simultaneously isn't `1` — it's however many `open()` calls are outstanding before the earlier ones close. EMFILE fires when that peak crosses `RLIMIT_NOFILE`.\n\nNothing in `run()` decides either side of that comparison:\n\n- **Demand** = fixture size × how many `run()` calls overlap. Vitest runs the nine importing test files in parallel workers, so the peak is the sum across workers, not per test. Only the tests with big `files` maps contribute enough to matter — which is why it's \"when a fixture declares a lot of files.\"\n- **Headroom** = the process fd limit, minus what's already held. A dev shell on macOS and a CI container have different `ulimit -n`, and CI worker count usually tracks available cores.\n- **Overlap** = scheduling. Node's fs promises queue onto the libuv threadpool (4 threads by default), so whether the first opens *close* before the last opens are *issued* depends on disk latency and how busy the box is. A fast warm local SSD drains the queue as fast as it fills and the peak stays low; a contended CI runner lets opens pile up and the peak is close to N.\n\nSo the same code, on the same input, crosses the threshold or doesn't depending on the machine and the moment — that's the intermittency. Locally all three factors point the safe way at once (fewer workers, higher limit, faster drain), which is why it never reproduces.\n\nTwo things also make the failure read worse than it is: `Promise.all` rejects on the first EMFILE without cancelling the rest, so the writes still in flight keep going and the error surfaces against whichever test lost the race; and the half-written `testDir` can make a *later* assertion fail with a confusing missing-file error instead of EMFILE at all.\n\n## 3. The bounded version\n\nTwo changes: cap in-flight work, and dedupe the `mkdir`s (many fixture files share a parent, so N `mkdir`s collapse to the unique-directory count, and the redundant concurrent `mkdir` on the same path goes away). `parentDirs` is created before the writes so every target directory exists first.\n\n```ts\nexport async function run(\n  title: string,\n  files: { [name: string]: string },\n  options: {\n    entry?: string[]\n    flags?: string[]\n    env?: Record<string, string>\n  } = {},\n) {\n  const testDir = path.resolve(cacheDir, filenamify(title))\n\n  // Write entry files on disk, at most FS_CONCURRENCY handles at a time.\n  const names = Object.keys(files)\n\n  // Thanks to `recursive: true`, this doesn't fail even if the directory already exists.\n  const parentDirs = [\n    ...new Set(names.map((name) => path.dirname(path.resolve(testDir, name)))),\n  ]\n  await mapLimit(parentDirs, FS_CONCURRENCY, (dir) =>\n    fsp.mkdir(dir, { recursive: true }),\n  )\n\n  await mapLimit(names, FS_CONCURRENCY, (name) =>\n    fsp.writeFile(path.resolve(testDir, name), files[name], 'utf8'),\n  )\n\n  // ...rest of run() unchanged\n}\n```\n\n```ts\n/**\n * A fixture's `files` map is caller-supplied, so an unbounded `Promise.all`\n * over it opens one file descriptor per entry. Past the process limit that\n * throws EMFILE, which depends on `ulimit -n` and on how many vitest workers\n * are writing at the same moment — not on this code. Cap the in-flight work\n * instead. Node's fs promises share a 4-thread libuv pool, so a cap of 8\n * already saturates it and costs nothing in wall clock.\n */\nconst FS_CONCURRENCY = 8\n\nasync function mapLimit<T>(\n  items: T[],\n  limit: number,\n  fn: (item: T) => Promise<unknown>,\n) {\n  let next = 0\n  const workers = Array.from(\n    { length: Math.min(limit, items.length) },\n    async () => {\n      while (next < items.length) {\n        await fn(items[next++])\n      }\n    },\n  )\n  await Promise.all(workers)\n}\n```\n\nThe remaining `Promise.all` is over `workers`, whose length is `Math.min(limit, items.length)` — bounded by construction, independent of the fixture. No new dependency; the fixture has no `p-limit`/`p-map` available, and `strict: true` without `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` makes `items[next++]` typed `T`.\n\n## 4. Verification\n\nI checked the fix against the detector in a scratch copy rather than in the fixture: baseline reproduced `unbounded_async_fanout::test/utils.ts::run → mkdir` (medium, 0.88); after the edit that fingerprint is absent and the file reports no findings at all, so nothing new was introduced. Scratch tree is at the path in `/tmp/fanout-check-dir` if you want to diff it.\n\n**I deliberately did not write this into `evals/fixtures/04-monorepo/test/utils.ts`.** Per `evals/README.md`, a fixture whose finding set changes is a re-baseline trigger, and recency decay means a fixture edited today ranks differently for 14 days — landing this would silently move eval numbers for a measurement input. If you do want it applied to the fixture, say so and I'll make the edit plus the results/baseline bookkeeping that goes with it.",
